## INTRODUCTION
Since the advent of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) in the late $1990^{\circ}\mathrm{s},{}^{1}$ these porous materials have shown promise for critical applications2 such as gas storage,3 catalysis,4 drug delivery,5 thermal energy storage,6 sensors,7 etc. MOFs have become benchmark materials for adsorption, surpassing most traditional adsorbents for hydrogen storage,3a methane storage, $^{3\mathrm{b}}\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separation,8 etc. By marrying the advantages of the inorganic and organic chemistries, MOFs exhibit both polyfunctionality and a high degree of tunability, as well as the opportunity to post-functionalize them.9 Among the main advantage of this tunability is the possibility to perform reticular chemistry1d, 10 through ligand functionalization, length/width $10\mathrm{{g}}$ variation, and/or metal ion substitution, which enables strategic design and synthesis of materials for specific applications.
A remaining challenge in MOF chemistry is the creation of mesoscale cavities, which are necessary for increased storage capacity, encapsulation of large molecules, etc. Reticular chemistry strategies to surpass previous limits on pore size and cavity diameter include increasing the distance between the organic molecular building blocks and using longer or wider organic ligands.10e, 11 However, many of the most well-known MOFs are associated to self-dual nets (pcu: MOF-5,1c IRMOFs series,1d MXFSIX MOFs12) or nets that can easily interpenetrate upon ligand elongation (tbo: HKUST-1,1e, 13 fcu: UiO-66,14 acs: MIL-88’s,15 etc.). To avoid this problem, researchers have developed platforms based on topologies that cannot interpenetrate, such as IRMOFs- $.74^{\mathrm{10e}}$ and rht-MOFs16, which, thanks to the design of very ambitious organic ligands, currently hold porosity records.10e, 11b, 11d
Regrettably, systematic access to MOFs exhibiting hierarchical porosity from the micro to the meso range remains a challenge, as it currently demands use of complex organic ligands that are not commercially available.11c, 17 Although MOFs were initially considered as structurally ideal, defect-free materials, numerous recent studies have highlighted the nonnegligible level of structural defects and irregularities in these materials, which strongly influence their porosity and catalytic properties.17e, 18 These findings suggest that access to hierarchically porous materials would require rational control over the structural defects within MOFs. Unfortunately, to date, only a few methods for generating and controlling such defects have emerged. One such strategy is to use large amounts of monotopic agents (known as modulators) in competition with the required polytopic ligand corresponding to the targeted MOFs.14b, 19 Alternatively, rational design strategies such as transversal reticular chemistry also have proven invaluable for generating MOFs with ordered defects.10g However, there are very few reports on engineering of defects and control of hierarchical porosity with commercially available and/or moderately sized ligands,17e, 18e, 20 and some of the existing methods require harsh chemical or thermal treatments.
Our group recently reported the first solid-gas phase covalent post-synthetic modification of a MOF: under mild conditions, we achieved quantitative conversion of the constituent olefin groups in single crystals of UiO-66-like MOF into trioxolane moieties, aldehydes and/or carboxylates without compromising the crystallinity.21 Encouraged by the stability of the MOFs under these conditions, we envisioned exploiting this solid-gas phase ozonolysis22 method to cleave and remove selected organic ligands within MOFs. We hypothesized and later confirmed (vide infra) that ligands containing non-terminal olefin groups could be split into several parts (Scheme S1). When implemented in MOFs, these ligands can be selectively broken and subsequently removed from the framework, to provide an original and controllable method for post-synthetic fusion of micropores into mesopores.
Herein we report application of our post-synthetic strategy to selectively and quantitatively cleave and remove the organic ligands in two multivariate (MTV)23 Zr-fcu-MOFs (Figure 1), thereby affecting their adsorption performance in gas uptake. By controlling the ozone inert/active ratio of ligands in these MOFs, we were able to control the final number of defects in their structures.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Zr-fcu-azo/sti system.
System validation. Our ligand-removal strategy is only amenable to MOFs based on ligand pairs that exhibit the following characteristics: similar shape and length; similar reactivity under given reaction conditions; and opposite reactivity to ozone (Figure 1b). To this end, we selected two dicarboxylic acids: $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -azobenzene dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{azo)}$ and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -stilbene dicarboxylic acid $\begin{array}{r l}{\lefteqn{\bigl(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{sti}\bigr)}}\end{array}$ (with lengths $\approx13.3$ Å). The reactivity of each ligand to ozone was initially tested in solution, in $N,N^{\prime}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF), which was used to avoid $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking of the ligands and to facilitate access of the ozone molecules to the olefin groups. Interestingly, the two acids showed diametric responses to ozone: $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{a}\mathbf{z}\mathbf{o}$ was unreactive, whereas $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{sti}$ was fully converted into terephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc)}$ and formylbenzoic acid (Hfba) (Scheme S1, Figure S18).
Having confirmed the suitability of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{a}\mathrm{z}\mathrm{o}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{sti}$ as a ligand pair, we then decided to choose an appropriate MOF for testing. We selected the robust $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-MOF platform, which had already proven amenable to post-synthetic modification and ozone resistant.9b, 9d, 21, 24 Thus, we embarked on the synthesis of the resultant MOFs (Zr-fcu-azo25 and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-sti26) and testing of their response to ozonolysis. Both MOFs were synthesized by suspending $\mathrm{\zr{Cl_{4}}}$ and the corresponding dicarboxylic ligand in DMF, in the presence of HCl and Lproline, and then heating the resulting mixture at $120~^{\mathrm{~o~C~}}$ overnight in a scintillation vial. The resulting crystals (of $Z\mathbf{r}.$ fcu-azo or $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-sti) were washed with DMF and then subjected to solvent exchange with acetone. As expected, $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms collected at $77~\mathrm{{K}}$ confirmed comparable Brunauer-Emmett-Teller areas $(\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{BET}})$ of $3025\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcuazo and $3065~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-sti (Figure 2a). When submitted to ozone treatment for $30~\mathrm{\min}$ , $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-azo retained its crystallinity and porosity, whereas $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-sti collapsed into an amorphous, non-porous solid due to cleavage of all the ligands of the framework (Figures 2a, S2, S9).
Ozonolysis of $\mathbf{z}\mathbf{r}$ -fcu-azo/sti- $.\mathbf{X\%}$ . To selectively break the sti ligands, we packed ca. $50\mathrm{mg}$ of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-azo/sti- $.X\%$ into a Lshaped glass tube, connected on one side to the ozonator (through a $\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$ humidity trap), and on the other side, to a vacuum pump (through a KI trap), to ensure a continuous flow of ozone through the column. The reaction was run at room temperature for $30~\mathrm{\min}$ . The ozonated $\mathrm{Zr{-}f c u{-}a z o/s t i{-}X^{0}/o}$ materials maintained their high crystallinity after ozonolysis, as confirmed by PXRD (Figures S2-S10). $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR of digested, Zr-fcu-azo/sti- $.X\%$ _OZ confirmed the full conversion of sti into bdc and fba for all samples (Figure S20, Table S4). The 1:1 bdc/fba ratio observed for all $\mathrm{Zr\mathrm{-}f c u\mathrm{-}a Z O/s t i\mathrm{-}X^{\%}\mathrm{-}O Z}$ also indicated the stoichiometric character of the reaction (Figures 2d, 3a). As expected, based on our preliminary results with $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - fcu-azo, the content of azo ligand in the framework was not affected by ozonolysis, thus confirming its high selectivity towards olefin bonds.
Porosity properties of washed, ozonated Zr-fcu-azo/sti$X\%$ . After confirming that in the washed, ozonated $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu$\mathrm{{azo/sti-X\%}}$ , all the sti ligands had been broken (Figure S20), most of the resulting organic fragments had washed out (Figure S21) and the high crystallinity had remained (Figure S10c), we submitted these materials to ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption tests at $77~\mathrm{K}$ (Figure 2f, Table 1). As expected, $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-azo/sti- $10\%$ performed very similarly to the pre-ozonated material, showing only slightly higher values for apparent $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ $3115\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ vs. $3060\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ and total pore volume $(1.32~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ vs. $1.28~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ ). Based on the Dubinin-Radushkevich (DR) equation,27 the relative proportion of micro-porosity to the total pore volume was estimated to be $96\%$ (Figure 2g). Although the washed ozonated $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcuazo/sti- $20\%$ showed a nearly identical value for this parameter $(95\ \%)$ , its isotherm exhibited a shoulder/step characteristic from bigger pores, giving insights into the modified nature of the microporosity. The isotherms for the washed ozonated Zrfcu-azo/sti- $30\%$ and $Z\mathbf{r}$ -fcu-azo/sti- $40\%$ did not show the type I shape of the isotherms of the corresponding starting MOFs, but rather they exhibited a small hysteresis on the desorption branch, characteristic of mesoporosity (Figures S29, S30). Notably, ozonolysis treatment reduced the apparent $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ : for the washed ozonated Zr-fcu-azo/sti- $30\%$ , $2680~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (vs. 3000 $\mathbf{m}^{2}/\mathbf{g})$ ; and for the washed ozonated $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-azo/sti- $40\%$ , 2580 $\mathbf{m}^{2}/\mathbf{g}$ (vs. $3040~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ (Table 1). However, in both cases, this decrease was offset by a major increase in the proportion of pores with mesoscale dimensions, as derived from the DR equation (Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ and Table S9): for the washed ozonated $Z\mathrm{r}.$ - fcu-azo/sti- $30\%$ , $15\%$ (vs. $2\%$ ); and for the washed ozonated $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-azo/sti- $40\%$ , $24\%$ (vs. $3\%$ ). Likewise, the total pore volume also increased in both cases: for the washed ozonated $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-azo/sti- $30\%$ , $1.36~\mathrm{cm^{3}/g}$ (vs. $1.24~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ ) and for the washed ozonated $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-azo/sti- $40\%$ , $1.44~\mathrm{cm^{3}/g}$ (vs. 1.27 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ . Similar results were observed in the washed ozonated $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-azo/sti- $50\%$ , which exhibited the most extreme change relative to the corresponding starting MOF: the contribution of the mesoporosity reached $42\%$ of the total porosity (Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ and Table S9), and the hysteresis characteristic from mesoporosity was clearly visible (Figure S31); consequently, its $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ was only $1860\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (vs. $3065\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . Note that additional washing treatment did not affect the sorption properties (Figure S40a), excluding the hypothesis of structural etching sometimes observed through chemical treatment.18e, 28
The modification of the micro- vs meso- porosity ratio was also reflected by studying the PSD (Figure 2i). If no noticeable change was observed for the washed ozonated $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-azo, a subtle appearance of pores of ca. $25\textup{\AA}$ and above was noted for the washed ozonated $Z\mathbf{r}$ -fcu-azo/sti- $10\%$ . The washed ozonated $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-azo/sti- $20\%$ and $30\%$ showed a clear decrease of the initial main pore size of ca. $18.4\mathring\mathrm{A}$ and an increase of the second population at ca. $22.4\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , confirming that micropores are merged into wider pores. For these two samples, the formation of pores bigger than $25\textup{\AA}$ was also visible. Finally, the initial main pores of ca. $18.4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ completely vanished in washed and ozonated $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - fcu-azo/sti- $40\%$ and $50\%$ . In these cases, the pores were mainly observed at ca. $22.4\mathring\mathrm{A}$ and spread over a wide range up to $50\textup{\AA}$ . Albeit non observed through PXRD, the pore population centered at $22.4\mathring\mathrm{A}$ could be attributed to the existence of nonordered reo motifs, resulting from the removal of inorganic clusters, as hypothesized in some comparable systems.18e
Zr-fcu-bpdc/pdac system.
Based on these pioneering results, we envisioned extending our method to a more challenging system. We considered that $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{bpdc)}$ and 1,4- phenylenediacrylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}p d a c)}$ , found in the two $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcuMOFs UiO-67 and NU-801, would be a suitable ligand pair, given their geometric (length $\approx11.3\mathring\mathrm{A}$ ) and chemical properties (vide supra). A particularity of H2pdac is its two olefin bonds (Figure 3b), which we reasoned would enable cutting of the ligand into three distinct parts, rather than two, as in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{sti}$ (Scheme S2, Figure S23). In addition, we hypothesized that removal of the central part of the ligand could generate terephthalaldehyde, which could be sublimated under vacuum at moderate temperature -for example, during ozonolysis and/or during a classical pre-sorption activation procedure (Figure 3). That turned out to be correct and, by obviating the washing step, represented a major improvement to our method.
Synthesis of $\mathbf{z}\mathbf{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $.\mathbf{X\%}$ . Multivariate $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcuMOFs containing specific ratios of bpdc/pdac, from 100:0 (UiO-67)14, 29 to 0:100 (NU-801)19b, 30 were prepared from mixtures of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ bpdc and $\mathrm{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{pdac}$ dissolved in DMF in the presence of trifluoroacetic acid, and heated at $120~\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ in a scintillation vial. The samples were washed with DMF overnight and then soaked in toluene for 3 days. The phase purity of the single crystals of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $.X\%$ $\mathrm{{X}}\approx10$ , 20, 30, $40\%$ pdac molar percentage) was confirmed by PXRD (Figure 4b), $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR of digested crystals (Figure S24, Table S7) and $\Nu_{2}$ sorption analysis performed at $77\mathrm{K}$ (Figure 4e and Table 2). The sorption isotherms for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $.X\%$ were comparable $(2210\ \mathrm{m^{2}/g}<\mathrm{A_{BET}}<2310\ \mathrm{m^{2}/g}$ and 0.92 $\mathrm{cm^{3}/g}<\mathrm{Vt}<0.96~\mathrm{cm^{3}/g}$ . We observed a slight shoulder at a $\mathrm{P/P}_{0}$ value of ca. 0.1, which we attributed to a higher number of initial defects in the structure, likely due to our use of a slightly different synthetic procedure than for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-azo/sti- $X\%$ . This is in fact reflected by the estimation of the PSD (Figure 4h), in which all samples presented two main contributions at ca. 15.2 $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ and $18.4\mathring\mathrm{A}$ ; the bigger size being attributed (similarly as for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-azo/sti system, vide supra) to modulator-related defects.
Ozonolysis of $\mathbf{z}\mathbf{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $.\mathbf{X\%}$ . Analogously to the previous MOF pair (vide supra), the crystallinity and porosity of UiO-67 were unaffected by ozonolysis (Figures 4a, S11), but cleavage of the pdac ligands in NU-801 led to collapse of its framework and loss of its crystallinity and porosity (Figures 4a, S16). Having demonstrated this, we proceeded with ozonolysis of the Zr-fcu-bpdc/pdac- $.X\%$ MOFs. $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ -NMR of all $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcubpdc/pdac- $.X\%$ _OZ highlighted the absence of any remaining pdac ligand (Figures S25, Table S8). Moreover, after the Zrfcu-bpdc/pdac- $.X\%\mathrm{{_{-}}O Z}$ were activated at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , a white solid was recovered in the cold trap (77 K) placed between the MOFs and the pump. This solid was confirmed by $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR to be terephthalaldehyde (Figure 4d), which, after cleavage of pdac, we were able to directly extract from the framework via sublimation, as anticipated.
Porosity of $\mathbf{z}_{\mathbf{r}}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $.\mathbf{X\%}$ _OZ. To investigate the effects of removing the central core of pdac on the porosity of the $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $.X\%$ _OZ, we performed ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption experiments on these MOFs (Figure 4f, Table 2). Upon extracting terephthalaldehyde from the backbone of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcubpdc/pdac- $10\%$ , the porosity was enhanced, as demonstrated by an increase in the apparent $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ (from $2295\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ to $2400\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ . In this sample, besides the evolution of microporous pore volume $(\mathrm{V_{\upmu}})$ (from $0.95\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ to $0.97\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ was not significant, a more pronounced shoulder in the isotherm reflected a transformation of the microporosity. The emergence of some mesoporosity was revealed by the DR equation, with a contribution of $11\%$ (vs. $1\%$ ) to the total porosity. In contrast, upon further removal of pdac pillars, from $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac$20\%\_\mathrm{OZ}$ and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $30\%$ _OZ, the apparent $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ decreased sharply: for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $20\%$ _OZ $(\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{BET}}\ =$ $1870~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ vs. $2210~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ and for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $.30\%$ _OZ $(\mathrm{A_{BET}}=1730\mathrm{m^{2}/g}$ vs. $2245\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\dot{}$ ). The loss in microporosity for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $20\%$ _OZ $\mathrm{(V)\upmu=0.73~cm^{3}/g}$ vs. $0.91~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ 0 and for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $30\%$ _OZ $(\mathrm{V_{\upmu}}=0.65~\mathrm{cm^{3}/g}$ vs. 0.93 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ was offset by a major increase in the proportion of pores with mesoscale dimensions, as derived from the DR equation (Figure $4\mathrm{g}$ , Table S10): for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $20\%$ _OZ, $26\%$ (vs. $4\%$ ) and for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $30\%$ _OZ, $38\%$ (vs. $1\%$ ). Likewise, the total pore volume also slightly increased in both cases: for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $20\%$ _OZ, $0.99\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ (vs. 0.95 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ and for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $30\%$ _OZ, $1.05\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ (vs. 0.94 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ . As expected, Zr-fcu-bpdc/pdac- $40\%$ , which, upon ozonation, lost its crystallinity (Figure S15) due to structural collapse caused by removal of $40\%$ of its ligands, was nonporous. Overall, the change in the porosity in $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac$X\%$ paralleled that of $\mathrm{Zr\mathbf{-fcu-a}Z o/s t i\mathbf{-}\mathbf{X}\%}.$ . However, in the former, these changes occurred at lower percentages of ligand removal—presumably due to a greater number of defects in the starting MOFs.
The estimation of the PSD in this system also confirmed the evolution of the porosity towards mesopores (Figure 4i). While ozonolysis of UiO-67 did not affect the pore system of the pristine MOF, the appearance of pores distributed between 21.6
Å and $27.4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ was clearly visible for the ozonated $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcubpdc/pdac- $10\%$ , $-20\%$ and $-30\%$ . In addition, the two initial main pore populations decreased in ozonated Zr-fcubpdc/pdac- $20\%$ and $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $30\%$ . In these two last samples, the porosity population was extended up to ca. ${\ }50\mathrm{\AA}.$ , further confirming the fusion of micropores into novel mesopores.
## CONCLUSIONS
In conclusion, we have developed a selective and quantitative method for post-synthetic modification of MOF porosity via ozonolysis. Through removal of organic ligands, our approach causes micropores to fuse into larger (mesoscale) pores in a controlled fashion and without any loss in total porosity. It selectively breaks the olefin bonds in organic ligands without affecting other organic moieties and can be applied for short reaction times (ca. $30\mathrm{\min}$ ) under mild conditions (room temperature). Moreover, we have demonstrated that ozonolysis can be used as a novel tool to selectively remove, and recover, specific fragments of molecules/frameworks through solid-gas manipulation.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
## Materials and Instruments
Reagents. $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -stilbenedicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{sti)}$ , $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyl dicarboxylic $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{bpdc)}$ , and azobenzene- $^{.4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{azo)}$ were purchased from TCI Europe. Acetic acid, 1,4- phenylenediacrylic $\mathrm{(H_{2}p d a c)}$ , trifluoroacetic acid (tfa), zirconium chloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4})}$ , L-proline, hydrochloric acid, potassium iodide and anhydrous granular $\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$ $2\cdot6\mathrm{mm}$ ) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (Merck). N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF) and acetone were obtained from Fisher Chemical. Toluene was purchased from Acros Organics. All the reagents and solvents were used as received. Pyrex tubes (ext $\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}\mathrm{~\pm~}$ ; in $\mathrm{~\normalfont~\leftmoon~}=\mathrm{~5.5~~mm}$ ; length $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}150\ \mathrm{\mm},$ were purchased from Vidrasa S.A. The tubes were bent into an Lshape with a flame torch.
Optical Microscopy images were captured by a Zeiss Axio Observer Z1m Optical Microscope.
PXRD diagrams were recorded on an X'Pert PRO MPD analytical diffractometer (Panalytical) at $45~\mathrm{kV}$ , $40~\mathrm{mA}$ using $\mathrm{CuK}\upalpha$ radiation $(\lambda=1.5406\mathring\mathrm{A}$ .
1H NMR spectra were recorded on a Bruker Avance $400{\mathrm{NMR}}\quad$ spectrometer (static magnetic field: 11.7 T) operating at Larmor frequencies of $400\mathrm{{MHz}}$ $(\mathrm{^{1}H})$ .
Ozonolysis treatment was performed using an ozone generator (N1668A from Ozonotec) generating a constant flow of 10.4 mmol/h at room temperature.
Volumetric $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms were collected at $77\mathrm{K}\left(\mathrm{N}_{2}\right)$ using an ASAP $2020~\mathrm{HD}$ (Micromeritics). Temperature was controlled by using a liquid nitrogen bath. Total pore volume (Vt) was calculated at $\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}=0.95$ , and microporous volume $(\mathrm{V_{\upmu}})$ was calculated using the Dubinin-Radushkevich equation. Pore size distribution was estimated using a density functional theory (DFT) model $\left(\Nu_{2}\right.$ – cylindrical pores – oxide surface) implemented in the Microactive 4.00 software with a regularization factor of 0.01.
Synthesis.
Synthesis of Zr-fcu-azo/sti- $.\mathbf{X\%}$ . $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -stilbenedicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}s t i,~0.44-x~m m o l)}$ and azobenzene- $^{.4,4^{,}}$ -dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{a}}z\mathbf{0}$ , x mmol) were suspended in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF and sonicated for $30~\mathrm{min}$ in a $23\mathrm{-mL}$ scintillation vial. Then $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of a DMF solution of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ ( $105~\mathrm{mg}$ , $0.45\mathrm{\mmol}$ ), L-proline $(250\mathrm{mg},2.17\mathrm{mmol})$ and $45\upmu\mathrm{L}$ conc. HCl were transferred into the vial and vortexed for 1 minute. After heating at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $16\mathrm{h}$ , the reaction afforded orange octahedral crystals of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -fcu$\mathrm{azo/sti-X\%}$ (see Supporting Information for more details about the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{azo}{:}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{sti}$ ratio in this synthesis).
Synthesis of $\mathbf{z}\mathbf{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $.\mathbf{X\%}$ . $\mathrm{\zr{Cl_{4}}}$ ( $200~\mathrm{{mg}}$ , 0.86 mmol), 1,4-phenylenediacrylic acid $\mathrm{\cdotH_{2}p d a c}$ , x mmol) and biphenyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{~\textmu~}_{2}\mathrm{bpdc}$ , y mmol) were dissolved by sonication in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF in the presence of trifluoroacetic acid (tfa, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ ) in a $23\mathrm{-mL}$ scintillation vial. After heating at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $72\mathrm{{h}}$ , colorless octahedral crystals of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $.X\%$ were obtained (see Supporting Information for more details about the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ bpdc/H2pdac ratio in the synthesis).
Sample activation.
Activation of Zr-fcu-azo/sti- $.\mathbf{X\%}$ . Crystals of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu$\mathrm{azo/sti-X\%}$ were soaked in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF (refreshed after $4\mathrm{h}$ ) and then subjected to solvent exchange in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of acetone (refreshed after $4\ \mathrm{~h~}$ ). The supernatant was removed after decantation, and the crystals were dried at $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Sample purity was verified by PXRD (Figure 2b), and experimental azo/sti ratios were analyzed by $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR of the digested samples (Figure S19, Table S3). Crystals of $\mathrm{Zr\mathrm{-}f c u\mathrm{-}a z o/s t i\mathrm{-}X\%}$ were then transferred into a glass sorption cell and activated under dynamic vacuum at $85~\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ prior to sorption experiments.
Activation of $\mathbf{z}_{\mathbf{r}}.$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $.\mathbf{X\%}$ . Crystals of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcubpdc/pdac- $.X\%$ were soaked in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF for 24 and then subjected to solvent exchange for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of toluene (refreshed twice per day). The supernatant was removed by filtration. The sample purity was verified by PXRD (Figure 4b), and the experimental bpdc/pdac ratios were analyzed by $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR of the digested samples (Figure S24, Table S7). Crystals of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $.X\%$ were then transferred into a glass sorption cell and activated under dynamic vacuum at $85^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ prior to sorption experiments.
Ozonolysis treatment.
Ozonolysis of the organic ligands. In a typical procedure, $30~\mathrm{\mg}$ of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{a}\mathrm{z}\mathrm{o}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{sti}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ bpdc or H2pdac were dissolved/dispersed in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF. The solution was then transferred to a $50\mathrm{mL}$ three-necked round bottom flask and was cooled to $0\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ using an ice-water bath. A constant ozone flux was blown into the solution through a glass pipette for 30 minutes under stirring. The crude reaction was then allowed to warm up to room temperature and the solvent was removed $i n$ vacuo. The crude residue was collected and analyzed by $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR. $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR analysis confirmed the inert character of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{a}\mathbf{z}\mathbf{o}$ and $\textstyle\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bpdc}$ , while revealing complete conversion, within 30 minutes, of the active linkers $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{sti}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ pdac.
Acetic acid wash.
About $50\mathrm{mg}$ of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-azo/sti- $X\%\_{\mathrm{{Z}}}$ were vortexed for a few seconds in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of a $0.5{\mathrm{~M~}}$ acetic acid solution in DMF and left at room temperature for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The supernatant was removed and analyzed by $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR after decantation (Figure S22, Table S6). The crystals were washed twice with $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of acetone. The acetone was removed after decantation, and the crystals were dried at $65~\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ . The sample purity was verified by PXRD (Figure S10c), and the experimental organic content was analyzed by $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR of the digested samples (Figures S21, Table S5). Crystals of washed ozonated $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-azo/sti- $.X\%$ were then transferred into a glass sorption cell and activated under dynamic vacuum at $85\ \mathrm{~\^~{~o~C~}~}$ prior to the sorption experiments. Note that the $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fcu-bpdc/pdac- $.X\%$ samples did not require this additional treatment to reveal their tuned porosity potential (Figures 3, S40b).
HF-Digestion methodology.
In a typical procedure, $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of MOF were placed in an Eppendorf tube, to which $120~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of a $5\%$ aq. HF solution were added. The mixture was sonicated for 15 minutes, and then the slurry was placed in a $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ sand bath inside a fume hood to evaporate overnight. The crude solid samples were highly soluble in DMSO- $\mathrm{d}^{6}$ without any need for addition of acid. Note that attempts with standard digestion methodologies were not successful, even when using the same quantities of HF and solvent. Therefore, the evaporation step is crucial for proper sample digestion. Moreover, the digested solutions obtained with our methodology did not require any specific (e.g. HFresistant) PTFE NMR tubes.
